:name: EX-D01 - a refusal nobody sent
:description: EX-G04 let the ground hear a refusal. Nobody authenticated it.

# Execution profile (design 4.3; rules G1/G2 of section 9.2). FIRST, so a profile that cannot be
# found takes the machines down loudly instead of leaving every node running with no quantum set.
$profile?=@~/space-cs-sim/scripts/profiles/interactive.resc
include $profile

# Every path hangs off $out, which has NO default - see any other scenario in this repository for
# why. Unset, LoadELF fails where you can see it.
#
#   renode -e '$out=@'"$(make -s out)" -e 'include @<this file>' -e start
$comm?=$out/build-comm-s01-sat0/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$obc?=$out/build-obc-d01-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$eps?=$out/build-eps-hard/zephyr/zephyr.elf
$injector?=@~/space-cs-sim/attacker/TcpCanInjector.cs

# The uplink into satellite 0 is AUTHENTICATED: this COMM verifies a CCSDS 355.0-B-2 MAC and an
# anti-replay counter that lives inside the signed portion, before anything else reads the frame.
# A plain frame is refused; a replayed one is refused; a tampered one is refused. `make sdls`
# measures all three.
#
# The crosslink is the same bus it was in EX-X01, and nothing about the two flags interacts. That
# is the exercise.
#
# Satellite 1's COMM is here so the crosslink has something on the far end. It is a real image with
# a real CSP address, not scenery: the two COMMs ping each other across the link at startup, which
# is how the exercise opens and how anyone reading the console can see the link carry a packet
# before seeing what else it carries.
$comm1?=$out/build-comm-s01-sat1/zephyr/zephyr.elf

$comm0uart?=$out/d01-sat0-comm.uart
$obc0uart?=$out/d01-sat0-obc.uart
$eps0uart?=$out/d01-sat0-eps.uart
$comm1uart?=$out/d01-sat1-comm.uart
$linkport?=3777
$xlinkport?=4577

# Three hubs. Two intra-spacecraft, kept apart by their names exactly as the constellation scenario
# keeps them apart - and one crosslink that deliberately joins the two COMMs. That last hub is the
# whole exercise: test_the_buses_are_isolated in tests/e2e/test_constellation.py asserts that the
# first two never see each other's traffic, and it still passes, because it runs the constellation
# scenario. This one adds a bus on purpose.
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub0"
emulation CreateCANHub "canHub1"
emulation CreateCANHub "crosslinkHub"

# --- satellite 0: the victim. Everything hardened. ------------------------------------------------

mach create "SAT0_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm0uart true
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal $linkport "spacelink0" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 spacelink0
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2

mach create "SAT0_OBC"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $obc
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $obc0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

mach create "SAT0_EPS"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub0
sysbus LoadELF $eps
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $eps0uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1

# --- satellite 1: the neighbour. Its COMM is on the crosslink; nothing else about it matters here.

mach create "SAT1_COMM"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/nucleo_h753zi.repl
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan1 canHub1
connector Connect sysbus.fdcan2 crosslinkHub
sysbus LoadELF $comm1
sysbus.usart3 CreateFileBackend $comm1uart true
logLevel 3 sysbus
logLevel 3 rcc
logLevel 3 fdcan1
logLevel 3 fdcan2

# --- the attacker ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A bare machine with no platform, no ELF and no CPU, exactly as EX-B01's: it exists so the hub can
# resolve GetName()/GetMachine() on the injector, because an ICAN attached to a hub must be a
# registered machine peripheral or the exception poisons the hub for every node on it.
#
# It sits on the CROSSLINK, not on either spacecraft's internal bus. That is the premise: one
# spacecraft in the constellation is already compromised, and what that buys is its radio. How it
# was compromised is another exercise's subject - EX-F01 ends with code execution on an OBC.
mach create "attacker"
include $injector
machine CreateTcpCanInjector "inj" $xlinkport
connector Connect inj crosslinkHub
